<p>&nbsp;The company provides an opportunity to turn unwanted technology into cash while supporting responsible electronic waste management. Instead of allowing old motherboards and PCBs to go unused, sellers can submit a list of their available items or send photographs for an initial assessment. The team evaluates the hardware and provides a price based on its potential value. Local customers may be able to arrange collection, while sellers outside the collection area can use prepaid shipping labels. The process is intended to avoid complicated paperwork and unnecessary delays, making it easier for individuals and businesses to sell their unused electronic components.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Selling old motherboards and PCBs also contributes to a more sustainable technology industry. Components that can be recovered or reused are given another opportunity to serve a purpose, while materials from unsuitable boards can be reclaimed through responsible recycling. This approach helps reduce electronic waste and makes better use of valuable resources that might otherwise be discarded.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The company aims to make selling printed circuit boards convenient, whether customers have a small number of items or a large quantity of electronic hardware. After receiving an enquiry, the team reviews the available boards and provides a valuation. Local sellers may be offered same-day or next-day collection, depending on availability, while customers outside the service area can receive prepaid labels to send their items without paying for shipping. The company aims to keep customers informed throughout the process, from collection or delivery through to payment, with clear communication and no hidden fees. Its stated approach is to pay the agreed quoted amount.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The service is designed for individuals, independent technicians, repair shops, IT companies, and other organisations with surplus motherboards and circuit boards. By offering consistent valuations and a straightforward selling process, the company aims to build long-term relationships with suppliers who regularly need to dispose of or sell unwanted electronic components. Whether customers have a single damaged board or a large stock of unused hardware, the service is intended to accommodate different requirements.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Responsible recycling is another important part of the company's approach. Motherboards and PCBs are assessed to determine whether they can be reused or refurbished. Hardware that cannot be reused is dismantled safely, and suitable materials are sent to recycling partners. The company states that its recycling approach follows recognised environmental standards and aims to recover useful materials while ensuring that harmful waste is handled appropriately.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Electronic waste can create environmental problems when it is disposed of carelessly. Certain materials and substances found in electronic components can harm the environment when they enter the soil or water. Through appropriate dismantling, material recovery, and recycling, the company aims to reduce the amount of electronic waste sent to landfill. Copper and other recoverable metals can be reclaimed, while potentially harmful substances are handled through responsible disposal processes. Selling unwanted boards therefore provides an opportunity to receive payment while supporting more sustainable electronic waste management.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The company highlights transparency and customer trust as important parts of its service. It states that it has worked with thousands of customers, including independent repair technicians, gamers, IT departments, and recyclers. Its aim is to provide fair valuations, prompt service, and a straightforward experience for both small and large sellers. Customers with individual damaged boards and businesses managing larger inventories can receive an approach suited to their requirements. The company emphasises clear communication, reliable payments, and building long-term relationships with the people and organisations it serves.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Customers who are ready to sell their old motherboards or PCBs can contact the company to begin the process. The website invites businesses and individuals to arrange a free collection by submitting their contact details, including their name, email address, telephone number, and location. The company can also be contacted directly by telephone on 0330 633 8577.</p>
<p>&nbsp;A motherboard is the main circuit board inside a computer and serves as the central connection point for its components. It links the processor, memory, storage devices, and external hardware, allowing the different parts of the system to communicate and work together. Without a motherboard, the other computer components cannot operate as a complete system.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Motherboards are manufactured in different sizes and configurations to suit desktop computers, laptops, servers, and embedded systems. Their designs continue to change as technology develops, with improvements to chipsets, data transfer systems, and connectivity. As newer models become available, older motherboards are often replaced, even when they are still functional. Understanding the purpose of a motherboard can help owners recognise its potential value when upgrading equipment or deciding what to do with an unused board.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The company accepts a range of motherboard types from desktop computers, laptops, industrial systems, gaming computers, and servers. Different motherboard form factors, including Mini-ITX and ATX, can be assessed, as can boards using different memory generations, such as DDR3 and DDR5. The condition of a board does not necessarily determine whether it has value. Even broken motherboards or those with missing components may contain materials and parts suitable for recovery or recycling.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Motherboards can contain small quantities of valuable metals, including copper, gold, and silver. These materials can be recovered through appropriate recycling processes, which means that a damaged or outdated board may still have value. Customers can send a photograph or the part number of a motherboard to help the team assess it and provide an indication of what it may be worth.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Old motherboards can remain valuable even after they become unsuitable for running modern computer systems. Some contain components and materials that can be reused or recycled, while particular models with uncommon chipsets may attract interest from collectors and people building older computers. There is also a market for refurbished computer parts, as people looking to repair or rebuild systems at a lower cost may prefer to purchase used hardware.</p>
<p>&nbsp;A motherboard that is no longer useful to its original owner may still be suitable for another person. Through resale, refurbishment, or recycling, old computer components can continue to serve a purpose rather than being discarded. The company's service aims to connect unwanted hardware with opportunities for reuse and material recovery, giving electronic components a useful life beyond their original application.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Instead of throwing away an outdated or unused motherboard, owners can consider selling or recycling it. Boards that remain functional may be resold or refurbished, while those that cannot be reused can be processed for material recovery. This helps extend the useful life of technology and reduces unnecessary waste. Recovered and reused components may also benefit local refurbishers, students, and hobbyists who use second-hand equipment for repairs, learning, and building computer systems.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Recycling printed circuit boards is important because PCBs are used in a wide range of electronic devices and can cause environmental problems when disposed of incorrectly. Some boards contain potentially harmful substances, including lead, mercury, and brominated flame retardants. When electronic waste is handled carelessly, these substances may enter the environment and affect soil, water, ecosystems, and human health.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Proper PCB recycling helps reduce these risks by recovering valuable materials and ensuring that harmful substances are handled appropriately. The company states that its boards are processed through certified facilities and that its approach follows a zero-landfill policy. Recycling also reduces the demand for newly mined resources by recovering metals already present in electronic equipment. Responsible processing therefore helps reduce waste and supports more sustainable electronic disposal practices.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The company's PCB recycling process begins with the inspection and sorting of incoming circuit boards. Boards that are still functional may be tested and refurbished for further use. Damaged or unsuitable boards are dismantled so that recoverable materials, including gold, silver, and copper, can be reclaimed. The company states that its recycling partners follow national and international electronic waste requirements and that it works with R2-certified and ISO-compliant recycling organisations.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Transparency is an important part of the stated recycling process. The company aims to make the handling of customers' electronic hardware clear, including whether boards are reused, refurbished, or sent for recycling. It states that materials are not sent to unauthorised facilities or simply dumped abroad. This approach is intended to give customers confidence that their unwanted circuit boards are being handled responsibly.</p>
